  [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. No change to how anything loads.
  [3mTrades:[0m keeps [38;5;153mindex.html[39m openable straight off disk and matches the file's current classic-script style — against a
  global and an implicit load-order dependency that every future form inherits. [3mWins when[0m you want zero friction now and
  don't want to introduce a serving requirement. [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m

  A and B share an architecture and differ only on the module story — but that's the decision every future form
  consumes, which is why it's a fork and not a detail.

  [1mC. Declarative delegated auto-tracking[0m
  One listener on [38;5;153mdocument[39m catches every form submit and records it from [38;5;153mdata-track-event[39m / [38;5;153mdata-track-fields[39m attributes
  on the [38;5;153m<form>[39m. Forms opt in through markup; no per-form JavaScript.
  [3mTrades:[0m adding tracking to form number five is one HTML attribute — against the fact that it can only see the submit,
  not the result. Your actual question is "who logged in," and success or failure is known only after [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns,
  so you'd still need an explicit call for outcomes and would end up maintaining both paths. [3mWins when[0m you have many
  similar forms and only care that they were submitted. [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mMy recommendation: A, or B if you'd rather not introduce a serving requirement.[0m C is the wrong shape for this goal
  specifically — it optimizes for the case where submission alone is the event, and you asked to track who [3mlogged in[0m,
  which is an outcome. Between A and B the architecture is identical, so the choice is only how much structure you want
  to buy now; B is a genuinely defensible answer for a fixture this small, and moving B to A later is a contained
  change.
